---
name: smoke-tests

description: |
  Errand verifying RustFS cluster is healthy and S3 API functions end-to-end.
  Probes /health/ready on every peer discovered via the rustfs-peer BOSH link, then
  performs a full S3 cycle — CreateBucket, PutObject, GetObject (content verify),
  DeleteObject, HeadObject (expect 404), DeleteBucket — against the first peer.

  Authentication uses AWS Signature Version 4, computed with openssl and od
  and sent with curl.  The HMAC is built in bash and fed to openssl on
  stdin, so neither the secret key nor any derived signing key appears on a
  command line.  Every x-amz-* header the errand sends is signed,
  because RustFS 1.0 rejects unsigned ones.  No packages are needed beyond the base
  tools that the ubuntu-noble and ubuntu-resolute stemcells ship (bash, curl,
  openssl, od, and date).

packages: []

templates:
  bin/run.erb: bin/run
  tls/ca.crt.erb: tls/ca.crt

consumes:
  - name: rustfs-peer
    type: rustfs-peer

properties:
  rustfs.access_key:
    description: |
      Admin access key for smoke-test S3 operations.
      Must match the rustfs.access_key set on the rustfs-server job.

  rustfs.secret_key:
    description: |
      Admin secret key for smoke-test S3 operations.
      Must match the rustfs.secret_key set on the rustfs-server job.

  rustfs.port:
    description: S3 API port. Must match the rustfs-server job's rustfs.port.
    default: 9000

  rustfs.region:
    description: |
      S3 region string to present in API requests.
      Must match the rustfs-server job's rustfs.region.
    default: us-east-1

  rustfs.tls.certificate:
    description: |
      API TLS certificate of the rustfs-server job.  The errand uses it only
      to decide the scheme, so when it is set, every request goes over
      https.  Leave it unset for a plaintext http cluster.  It must mirror
      the rustfs-server job's rustfs.tls.certificate.
    default: ""

  rustfs.tls.ca:
    description: |
      CA certificate that signed the rustfs-server API certificates.  When
      it is set, the errand verifies every node against it, by the node's
      link address.  When TLS is on and this is unset, the errand skips
      verification with -k and prints a warning, which only makes sense
      for a single node with a certificate from no shared CA.
    default: ""

  smoke_tests.timeout_secs:
    description: curl --max-time value in seconds for each request.
    default: 30
